            <title>[LU-21151] LFU: lfu-purge, a daemon that frees space in Fast Tier OST and PCC-RO caches</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21151</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;Fast Tier OSTs and PCC-RO caches hold copies of data that can be dropped when&lt;br/&gt;
space runs low. Nothing reclaims them by policy today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add &lt;tt&gt;lfu-purge&lt;/tt&gt;, as described in the lfu-purge HLD by Andreas:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;One process per cache keeps free space and free inodes between &lt;tt&gt;free_min&lt;/tt&gt; and &lt;tt&gt;free_max&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;A scanner thread finds candidates with LFU scans: &lt;tt&gt;llapi_scan_device()&lt;/tt&gt; on a Fast Tier OST, &lt;tt&gt;llapi_scan_namespace()&lt;/tt&gt; on a PCC-RO dataset. It keeps them in a weighted-age histogram, so the main thread can purge from the old end without waiting for a scan.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The scan rate follows free space.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Policy classes use &lt;tt&gt;lfs find&lt;/tt&gt; predicates, with weights and &lt;tt&gt;used_min&lt;/tt&gt;/&lt;tt&gt;used_max&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Before a purge, each candidate is checked again: its age, pins, opens, and for a fast mirror that another mirror is in sync.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;For a striped component, the OST with the first stripe owns the purge.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;Also add &lt;tt&gt;llapi_mirror_split()&lt;/tt&gt;: the daemon must drop a mirror as &lt;tt&gt;lfs mirror split -d&lt;/tt&gt; does, and that code is static in &lt;tt&gt;lfs.c&lt;/tt&gt; now.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Phase 1 of the HLD: on an OSS, the daemon needs a client mount. Setting up PCC-RO from &lt;tt&gt;mount -o pcc&lt;/tt&gt; waits for &lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21082" title="PCC-RO: add 'mount.lustre -o pcc=$CACHEDIR' option " class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-21082"&gt;LU-21082&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
